back.content:
== compares REFERENCES (memory addresses)
.equals() compares CONTENT (character by character)

Example:
String a = new String("hello");
String b = new String("hello");
a == b      → false (different objects in heap)
a.equals(b) → true  (same characters)

String literals are interned (String Pool):
String c = "hello";
String d = "hello";
c == d → true (same reference from pool)

Best practice: Always use .equals() for String comparison!

Short-circuit: Java stops evaluating as soon as the result is certain.

&& (AND): If left side is false → skip right side (result is always false)
|| (OR):  If left side is true  → skip right side (result is always true)

Practical use:
if (user != null && user.isActive()) { ... }
// Safe — if user is null, isActive() is never called

if (cache != null || loadFromDB()) { ... }
// loadFromDB() only called if cache is null

Benefit: Prevents NullPointerException and improves performance.

Syntax: condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse

It's a compact form of if-else that returns a value.

Example:
int age = 20;
String status = (age >= 18) ? "Adult" : "Minor";
// status = "Adult"

Another:
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;

Best for: simple one-liner value assignments.
Avoid: nested ternaries — they hurt readability.
Never use ternary for side effects (method calls that do work).

break   → Completely exits the loop (stops all iterations)
continue → Skips the rest of the CURRENT iteration, moves to next

Example:
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) break;     // stops at 3
    System.out.println(i); // prints 0,1,2
}

for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    if (i == 3) continue;  // skips 3
    System.out.println(i); // prints 0,1,2,4
}

break also exits switch statements.

The String Pool is a special area in the Java heap (PermGen/Metaspace) where the JVM caches String literals.

When you write: String s = "hello";
JVM checks if "hello" already exists in the pool.
  → YES: returns the same reference
  → NO: creates new String in pool, returns reference

This means:
String a = "hello";
String b = "hello";
// a and b point to the SAME object — memory saved!

new String("hello") always creates a NEW heap object (bypasses pool).

Use String.intern() to manually add to pool.

