while — tests condition BEFORE execution:
int i = 10;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i); // NEVER executes
}

do-while — tests condition AFTER execution (always runs at least once):
int i = 10;
do {
    System.out.println(i); // Prints 10
} while (i < 5);

Use do-while when:
• You need to execute the body at least once
• Classic example: menu-driven programs (show menu, then validate input)

do {
    // show menu, get user choice
} while (choice != 0);

Fall-through: In a switch, once a case matches, execution continues into ALL subsequent cases until a break or end of switch.

int day = 2;
switch(day) {
    case 1: System.out.println("Mon");
    case 2: System.out.println("Tue"); // matches here
    case 3: System.out.println("Wed"); // also executes!
    default: System.out.println("?");  // also executes!
}
// Prints: Tue, Wed, ?

Prevention: Add 'break' after each case.
This behavior can be useful: grouping multiple cases:
case 1:
case 2:
case 3: System.out.println("First 3 days"); break;
